@page "/"
@rendermode InteractiveServer

<PageTitle>War on Wolves</PageTitle>

<h1>War on Wolves</h1>

<button class="btn btn-primary" @onclick="TogglePerspective">
    <p class="mb-0">Learn about the
        @if (showEuropeanPerspective)
        {
            <span>Native American</span>
        }
        else
        {
            <span>European settler</span>
        }
        perspective
    </p> 
</button>

@if (showEuropeanPerspective)
{
    <section>
        <h2>European Settlers in North America</h2>
        <p>European settlers brought their own cultural attitudes about predatory species such as wolves with them from their homeland(s) to the New World. The driving force behind this hostile relationship between humans and wolves was the fight over natural resources in the form of livestock [<NavLink href="#1">1</NavLink>]. For the settlers, their horses, cattle, sheep, and pigs were critical sources of food and transportation. Likewise, the wolves saw these livestock as valuable prey due to the relative ease of hunting docile farm animals.</p>
        <p>Compared to Native American tribes, the European settlers were more dependent on livestock because they brought their domesticated animals with them from Europe in what is known as the <strong>Columbian exchange</strong>. Namely, horses, cattle, pigs, sheep, and chickens were introduced to North America by the European settlers [<NavLink href="#2">2</NavLink>].</p>
        <p>In addition, wolves were also seen as a danger to human safety by various European cultures. For example, the iconic fairy tale of <em>Little Red Riding Hood</em>-famously a work of anti-wolf propaganda- has its origins in pre-17th-century European folk tales ranging from France, Italy, and German-speaking regions.</p>
        <p>Due to these cultural factors, wolves were notably eradicated to the point of near extinction across the European continent by the 19th and 20th centuries [<NavLink href="#3">3</NavLink>]. Similarly, in North America, European settlers were primarily responsible for hunting wolves in the name of protecting their livestock as well as themselves against this predator species.</p>
        <img src="https://www.pbs.org/wnet/nature/files/2008/11/610_lobo_wolf-wars.jpg" alt="A group of men sitting in front of the corpses of hunted wolves" />
        <p>As a result of relentless overhunting and prolific anti-wolf cultural artifacts, nearly the entire nationwide gray wolf population was exterminated in the lower 48 states by the mid 20th century [<NavLink href="#1">1</NavLink>].</p>
    </section>
}
else
{
    <section>
        <h2>Native American Tribes</h2>
        <p>There are hundreds of different Native American tribes that are indigenous to the North American region. Much like how the settlers with "European" origins should not necessarily be considered to be a cultural monolith, not every Native American tribe perceived wolves the same way in their own traditional cultural beliefs.</p>
    </section>
}


<section>
    <h2>Bibliography</h2>
    <ol>
        <li id="1">
            PBS. “The Wolf That Changed America | Wolf Wars: America’s Campaign to Eradicate the Wolf | Nature | PBS.” Nature, 27 Oct. 2014, www.pbs.org/wnet/nature/the-wolf-that-changed-america-wolf-wars-americas-campaign-to-eradicate-the-wolf/4312/.
        </li>
        <li id="2">
            Crosby, Alfred. “The Columbian Exchange | Gilder Lehrman Institute of American History.” Gilderlehrman.org, 2025, www.gilderlehrman.org/history-resources/essays/columbian-exchange.
        </li>
        <li id="3">
            “The Return of the Wolf in Europe.” World Wildlife Fund, 5 July 2021, www.worldwildlife.org/news/stories/the-return-of-the-wolf-in-europe/.
        </li>
    </ol>
</section>

@code {
    private bool showEuropeanPerspective = true;

    private void TogglePerspective()
    {
        showEuropeanPerspective = !showEuropeanPerspective;
    }
}